  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nScattered showers and storms are moving across areas mainly \nnear and east of the James River this afternoon, with some \nclearing occurring behind this activity. This should allow for \ngreater destabilization in south-central South Dakota this \nafternoon and thus have issued a Severe Thunderstorm Watch for \nBrule, Gregory, and Charles Mix Counties. Damaging winds to 65 \nmph and quarter sized hail are the main threats locally through \nthe early evening hours. The greater severe weather risks looks\nto be south and west of our area where the better instability \nlies, but still be sure to remain weather aware. Elsewhere, the\nenvironment has been pretty worked over and thus severe weather\nis less likely. The one exception is over southwest Minnesota \nwhere we've been able to warm to the low to middle 80s out ahead\nof this early afternoon activity. However, with the current \nrain and clouds moving in and growing increasingly thicker in\nthose areas, the threat for severe weather is diminishing there\nas well. There's also some clearing over north-central South \nDakota, so this will be another area to watch for storm \ndevelopment, with any activity there moving east-southeast \nthrough the evening into tonight. \n\nHeading into the night, the main wave will close off into an upper \nlevel low, with a surface low developing east of I-29 by daybreak \nFriday. This will all combine to keep rain and some storms in the \nforecast tonight through Friday morning and potentially as late as \nFriday afternoon near and east of I-29 as moisture wraps around the \nlow. Any severe threat will greatly diminish after midnight, so this \nwill transition to mostly a heavy rain threat as showers and storms \nwill continue to push through the area. This could lead to localized \nflash flooding primarily in urban and flood prone areas, so be sure \nto remain weather aware. For the good majority of the area though, \nthis should be beneficial rainfall, with additional totals generally \nbetween a half an inch to two inches through Friday afternoon. \nLocally higher pockets of 4+ inches remain possible, but that will \nbe for areas that see multiple thunderstorms move over that same \narea. With recent dryness, those rainfall totals won't be much of a \nconcern for rural areas. But urban areas could see some flash \nflooding with that kind of rainfall, so that will be the main\nconcern tonight. Another thing to note is that the aforementioned\nsurface low will be deepening over the area, so it will be a \nbit breezy on Friday, with gusts to around 35 mph possible. \nIsolated pockets of 40+ mph wind gusts can't be ruled out mainly\neast of I-29 Friday afternoon, but doesn't look like it will be\nwidespread enough to warrant wind headlines at this time. \n\nWe'll dry things out heading into Friday night, and depending on how \nquickly clouds clear out, we could see widespread lows in the \n50s by Saturday morning! If clouds take a little bit longer, \nwe'll still likely see some 50s west of I-29, it's just that \nplaces east of there where clouds clear the latest may only drop\nto the lower 60s. So still much cooler than we have been! Dry \nand sunny conditions will be in store for the weekend, with a \npleasant Saturday expected with highs only in the upper 70s to \nlower 80s across most of the area. Southerly flow returns on \nSunday, making for it to be a little hotter, though not nearly \nas hot as it has been. Only south-central South Dakota has a \nshot of reaching 90 degrees on Sunday, but heat indices there \nwill likely not stray too far from the air temperature. After \nFriday, our next chance of appreciable rain/storms will be \nMonday night into Tuesday as a cold front moves through the \narea. Not a lot of time to look into details given the focus on \ntoday and tonight's forecast, but it does look like there will \nbe enough instability and shear to at least have some strong to \nsevere thunderstorm risk, though details remain low. It does \nlook likely though that another round of cooler air will filter \nin behind this front, making for an overall pleasant first week \nof August.",
